=== Default scanning without path arguments <_default_scanning_without_path_arguments>

Whenever the program is run without any path arguments, the current working directory is scanned and it’s expected to be a project. The scanning strategy will attempt to find both build system script and manifest to determine the project structure.

==== Project discovery <_project_discovery>

Project discovery is performed by looking at both script and manifest files, considering. It’ll attempt find the nearest script either in the current working directory or any of its parent directories, and then look for a manifest to determine for complementary information, such as: local dependencies and their paths, bundled paths, project name, etc.

- If there are local dependencies, they can be linted optionally by passing #raw("--deps").
- Paths listed are entirely for supporting information, as sometimes paths or even the manifest isn’t present, or there are no paths listed, so the project discovery is still valid and strictly focused on the build script first.

Based on the build script discovery, the behavior defaults to only public modules being scanned. Executables and test modules are discovered but excluded by default.

#adoc-block-title[Reachability notes:]
- Traversal is recursive across imported files, so multi-hop public chains are included.
- Imports reachable only through non-public declarations are excluded.
- Package imports (for example #raw("@import(\"std\")")) are not treated as local lint targets.
- #raw("build.zig") and files under #raw("build/") are ignored by default.
  - Instead of files within the build directory, it’s mostly all of those files that are used/imported by the main build script (#raw("build.zig")).
- This avoids false-positive API checks from build tooling paths that are commonly present in #raw("build.zig.zon") #raw(".paths").
- CLI users can opt in with #raw("--include-build-scripts").

==== Scanning with arguments <_scanning_with_arguments>

When explicit paths are passed, each argument is treated as a file or directory target.

#adoc-block-title[Supported cases include:]
- A single file.
- A single directory.
- A mix of files and directories.

When explicit paths are provided, the default project discovery is disabled and only the specified targets are scanned.

#adoc-block-title[Path validity]
- Explicit paths are validated strictly by default.
- If a path does not exist, cannot be accessed, or refers to a non-Zig source file, an error is emitted and the process exits with a non-zero status.
- If a directory is valid but does not yield any Zig lint targets, a warning is emitted and that directory is skipped.
- If all explicit targets are valid but none yield any Zig lint targets, the process exits with a non-zero status.

#adoc-block-title[Path resolution]
- If the target is a Zig source file, it is treated as a module root candidate.
- If the target is a directory, module roots are resolved within that directory.
- For each resolved source file or directory, the module kind is determined:
  - A file containing a public #raw("main()") is treated as an executable root module.
  - A file containing a public #raw("build()") is treated as a build script root module.
  - Otherwise, it is treated as a library source file.
- In all cases, the publicly reachable API is traversed starting from the resolved module root.

== Linting rules <_linting_rules>

To check the rules documentation, check #link("https://jassielof.github.io/docent/lib/#docent.rules")[library documentation rules namespace] for the actual implemented rules.

== Formatting <_formatting>

#raw("docent fmt") formats explicit command-line paths first. When no paths are given,
it uses #raw("[fmt].include") from #raw(".config/docent.toml"); when that is also unset, it
uses #raw(".paths") from the nearest #raw("build.zig.zon"). Manifest-derived files are
limited to #raw(".zig") and #raw(".zon"), while manifest-derived directories are walked
recursively with the same filter. Entries such as README and license files are
therefore ignored, as are local path dependencies unless they are selected
explicitly. If a manifest has no #raw(".paths") field, its project directory is used
as the filtered recursive root.

Explicit file arguments remain strict: requesting a non-Zig file attempts to
format that file and reports parsing errors rather than silently ignoring a
likely typo.

In minimal output, the dedicated #raw("check cogni") and #raw("check cyclo") commands show
their raw #raw("score/threshold") pair (for example, #raw("30/15")) instead of repeating the
rule selected by the command. Aggregate #raw("check all") output keeps rule IDs so
every finding remains attributable. Complexity is an unbounded count, so
Docent does not present it as a percentage.

#raw("docent check all") ends its full diagnostic report with both the overall
warning/error total and a per-category, per-rule count breakdown. This keeps
the aggregate command convenient for CI while making the responsible checks
explicit.

